{
  "slug": "official-opening-marks-the-next-chapter-in-queensland-eye-institutes-history",
  "title": "Official opening marks the next chapter in Queensland Eye Institute’s history",
  "date": "2024-08-29",
  "modified": "2025-01-23",
  "categories": [
    "Events",
    "QEI Foundation"
  ],
  "hero": "wp-content/uploads/2024/08/20240820OfficialOpeningQEI15-1430x953.jpg",
  "hero_alt": "",
  "excerpt": "Another milestone in the Queensland Eye Institute Foundation's long history of saving sight is marked by the official opening of its new home",
  "body_html": "<p>The Queensland Eye Institute Foundation has marked the latest milestone in its long history of saving sight, with Her Excellency the Honourable Doctor Jeannette Young AC PSM, Governor of Queensland, officially opening its new home at 87 Ipswich Road Woolloongabba on 20th August 2024.</p>\n<figure aria-describedby=\"caption-attachment-5327\" class=\"wp-caption aligncenter\" id=\"attachment_5327\" style=\"width: 2000px\"><img alt=\"A woman in a red suit delivers a speech from behind a lecturn with an audience of men and women in suits looking on. There are large artworks on the wall behind the speaker.\" class=\"size-full wp-image-5327\" decoding=\"async\" fetchpriority=\"high\" height=\"1500\" id=\"longdesc-return-5327\" longdesc=\"https://qei.org.au?longdesc=5327&amp;referrer=5312\" sizes=\"(max-width: 2000px) 100vw, 2000px\" src=\"../../wp-content/uploads/2024/08/20240820OfficialOpeningQEI_30.jpg\" srcset=\"../../wp-content/uploads/2024/08/20240820OfficialOpeningQEI_30.jpg 2000w, ../../wp-content/uploads/2024/08/20240820OfficialOpeningQEI_30-300x225.jpg 300w, ../../wp-content/uploads/2024/08/20240820OfficialOpeningQEI_30-1024x768.jpg 1024w, ../../wp-content/uploads/2024/08/20240820OfficialOpeningQEI_30-768x576.jpg 768w, ../../wp-content/uploads/2024/08/20240820OfficialOpeningQEI_30-1536x1152.jpg 1536w, ../../wp-content/uploads/2024/08/20240820OfficialOpeningQEI_30-1430x1073.jpg 1430w, ../../wp-content/uploads/2024/08/20240820OfficialOpeningQEI_30-800x600.jpg 800w, ../../wp-content/uploads/2024/08/20240820OfficialOpeningQEI_30-400x300.jpg 400w\" tabindex=\"-1\" width=\"2000\"/><figcaption class=\"wp-caption-text\" id=\"caption-attachment-5327\">Her Excellency the Honourable Dr Jeannette Young AC PSM</figcaption></figure>\n<p>Following formalities, past and present staff members mingled with supporters in the foyer, before touring the research laboratories and expansive new clinical areas. Special guests included Justice Debra Mullins, President of the Queensland Court of Appeal and Chair of the Sylvia &amp; Charles Viertel Charitable Foundation, past CEO Professor Lawrie Hirst and His Excellency Mr. Radu Safta, Romanian Ambassador to Australia.</p>\n<figure aria-describedby=\"caption-attachment-5328\" class=\"wp-caption aligncenter\" id=\"attachment_5328\" style=\"width: 2000px\"><img alt=\"A group of men and women in business attire stand in a row in front of a brass plaque, mounted on a board with an open curtain on each side\" class=\"size-full wp-image-5328\" decoding=\"async\" height=\"1333\" id=\"longdesc-return-5328\" longdesc=\"https://qei.org.au?longdesc=5328&amp;referrer=5312\" sizes=\"(max-width: 2000px) 100vw, 2000px\" src=\"../../wp-content/uploads/2024/08/20240820OfficialOpeningQEI15.jpg\" srcset=\"../../wp-content/uploads/2024/08/20240820OfficialOpeningQEI15.jpg 2000w, ../../wp-content/uploads/2024/08/20240820OfficialOpeningQEI15-300x200.jpg 300w, ../../wp-content/uploads/2024/08/20240820OfficialOpeningQEI15-1024x682.jpg 1024w, ../../wp-content/uploads/2024/08/20240820OfficialOpeningQEI15-768x512.jpg 768w, ../../wp-content/uploads/2024/08/20240820OfficialOpeningQEI15-1536x1024.jpg 1536w, ../../wp-content/uploads/2024/08/20240820OfficialOpeningQEI15-1430x953.jpg 1430w, ../../wp-content/uploads/2024/08/20240820OfficialOpeningQEI15-800x533.jpg 800w, ../../wp-content/uploads/2024/08/20240820OfficialOpeningQEI15-400x267.jpg 400w\" tabindex=\"-1\" width=\"2000\"/><figcaption class=\"wp-caption-text\" id=\"caption-attachment-5328\">Her Excellency the Honourable Dr Jeannette Young AC PSM (centre) with Queensland Eye Institute Foundation Directors (L-R) Prof Mark Radford, Jane Prentice, Chair Mark Sheridan, Jemma Elder, Mario Pennisi, John Lowes</figcaption></figure>\n<figure aria-describedby=\"caption-attachment-5329\" class=\"wp-caption aligncenter\" id=\"attachment_5329\" style=\"width: 2000px\"><img alt='A woman in a red suit stands surrounded by a group of 17 men and women in blue medical scrubs in front of a wall with a sign reading \"Queensland Eye Institute\"' class=\"size-full wp-image-5329\" decoding=\"async\" height=\"1125\" id=\"longdesc-return-5329\" longdesc=\"https://qei.org.au?longdesc=5329&amp;referrer=5312\" sizes=\"(max-width: 2000px) 100vw, 2000px\" src=\"../../wp-content/uploads/2024/08/20240820OfficialOpeningQEI25.jpg\" srcset=\"../../wp-content/uploads/2024/08/20240820OfficialOpeningQEI25.jpg 2000w, ../../wp-content/uploads/2024/08/20240820OfficialOpeningQEI25-300x169.jpg 300w, ../../wp-content/uploads/2024/08/20240820OfficialOpeningQEI25-1024x576.jpg 1024w, ../../wp-content/uploads/2024/08/20240820OfficialOpeningQEI25-768x432.jpg 768w, ../../wp-content/uploads/2024/08/20240820OfficialOpeningQEI25-1536x864.jpg 1536w, ../../wp-content/uploads/2024/08/20240820OfficialOpeningQEI25-1430x804.jpg 1430w, ../../wp-content/uploads/2024/08/20240820OfficialOpeningQEI25-800x450.jpg 800w, ../../wp-content/uploads/2024/08/20240820OfficialOpeningQEI25-400x225.jpg 400w\" tabindex=\"-1\" width=\"2000\"/><figcaption class=\"wp-caption-text\" id=\"caption-attachment-5329\">Her Excellency the Honourable Dr Jeannette Young AC PSM meets with members of the QEI clinical support team</figcaption></figure>\n<h2>A long history of funding vision research</h2>\n<p>The Queensland Eye Institute Foundation has come a long way since 1965, when Dr John Ohlrich and supporters harnessed the fundraising powers of The Lions Clubs to raise money for vision research.</p>\n<figure aria-describedby=\"caption-attachment-5339\" class=\"wp-caption aligncenter\" id=\"attachment_5339\" style=\"width: 1854px\"><img alt=\"Old black and white photo of a group of men in suits talking to each other, some raising glasses in celebration\" class=\"size-full wp-image-5339\" decoding=\"async\" height=\"1236\" id=\"longdesc-return-5339\" loading=\"lazy\" longdesc=\"https://qei.org.au?longdesc=5339&amp;referrer=5312\" sizes=\"auto, (max-width: 1854px) 100vw, 1854px\" src=\"../../wp-content/uploads/2024/08/Original-Directors-Foundation-for-the-Prevention-of-Blindness.jpg\" srcset=\"../../wp-content/uploads/2024/08/Original-Directors-Foundation-for-the-Prevention-of-Blindness.jpg 1854w, ../../wp-content/uploads/2024/08/Original-Directors-Foundation-for-the-Prevention-of-Blindness-300x200.jpg 300w, ../../wp-content/uploads/2024/08/Original-Directors-Foundation-for-the-Prevention-of-Blindness-1024x683.jpg 1024w, ../../wp-content/uploads/2024/08/Original-Directors-Foundation-for-the-Prevention-of-Blindness-768x512.jpg 768w, ../../wp-content/uploads/2024/08/Original-Directors-Foundation-for-the-Prevention-of-Blindness-1536x1024.jpg 1536w, ../../wp-content/uploads/2024/08/Original-Directors-Foundation-for-the-Prevention-of-Blindness-1430x953.jpg 1430w, ../../wp-content/uploads/2024/08/Original-Directors-Foundation-for-the-Prevention-of-Blindness-800x533.jpg 800w, ../../wp-content/uploads/2024/08/Original-Directors-Foundation-for-the-Prevention-of-Blindness-400x267.jpg 400w\" tabindex=\"-1\" width=\"1854\"/><figcaption class=\"wp-caption-text\" id=\"caption-attachment-5339\">Dr John Ohlrich (left) with founding members of Foundation for the Prevention of Blindness, Queensland Division</figcaption></figure>\n<p>At that time there was only a handful of ophthalmologists in Queensland and eye health education was limited. John believed research, teaching and improving the delivery of eyecare could save the sight of Queenslanders, whose vision was threatened by preventable and treatable eye disease.</p>\n<p>His son Stephen recalls one Lions Club meeting, where John announced ‘I will sell 100 dozen Lions’ Christmas cakes. What are the rest of you going to do?’. Stephen, who was a medical student at the time, says he delivered those cakes to Brisbane ophthalmology practices in his rusty Holden Ute.</p>\n<figure aria-describedby=\"caption-attachment-5334\" class=\"wp-caption aligncenter\" id=\"attachment_5334\" style=\"width: 2000px\"><img alt=\"Four women are in a marquee with tables covered in Christmas decorations and items for sale\" class=\"size-full wp-image-5334\" decoding=\"async\" height=\"1333\" id=\"longdesc-return-5334\" loading=\"lazy\" longdesc=\"https://qei.org.au?longdesc=5334&amp;referrer=5312\" sizes=\"auto, (max-width: 2000px) 100vw, 2000px\" src=\"../../wp-content/uploads/2024/08/Fundraising-stall.jpg\" srcset=\"../../wp-content/uploads/2024/08/Fundraising-stall.jpg 2000w, ../../wp-content/uploads/2024/08/Fundraising-stall-300x200.jpg 300w, ../../wp-content/uploads/2024/08/Fundraising-stall-1024x682.jpg 1024w, ../../wp-content/uploads/2024/08/Fundraising-stall-768x512.jpg 768w, ../../wp-content/uploads/2024/08/Fundraising-stall-1536x1024.jpg 1536w, ../../wp-content/uploads/2024/08/Fundraising-stall-1430x953.jpg 1430w, ../../wp-content/uploads/2024/08/Fundraising-stall-800x533.jpg 800w, ../../wp-content/uploads/2024/08/Fundraising-stall-400x267.jpg 400w\" tabindex=\"-1\" width=\"2000\"/><figcaption class=\"wp-caption-text\" id=\"caption-attachment-5334\">2005 Fundraising at Brisbane’s Christmas market by the Prevent Blindness Foundation, now called the Queensland Eye Institute Foundation</figcaption></figure>\n<h2>Generous donors allow for expansion</h2>\n<p>Since then, the Foundation has explored different ways to deliver on its commitment to save sight. In 1986, supported by a generous donation from local businessman, <a href=\"../why-viertels-vision/index.html\">Charles Viertel</a>, it established a Chair of Ophthalmology at the University of Queensland.</p>\n<figure aria-describedby=\"caption-attachment-5332\" class=\"wp-caption aligncenter\" id=\"attachment_5332\" style=\"width: 1804px\"><img alt=\"Close up of two men in suits, one looking away and talking, in front of a brass plaque\" class=\"wp-image-5332 size-full\" decoding=\"async\" height=\"1202\" id=\"longdesc-return-5332\" loading=\"lazy\" longdesc=\"https://qei.org.au?longdesc=5332&amp;referrer=5312\" sizes=\"auto, (max-width: 1804px) 100vw, 1804px\" src=\"../../wp-content/uploads/2024/08/Charles-Viertel-and-Lawrie-Hirst.jpg\" srcset=\"../../wp-content/uploads/2024/08/Charles-Viertel-and-Lawrie-Hirst.jpg 1804w, ../../wp-content/uploads/2024/08/Charles-Viertel-and-Lawrie-Hirst-300x200.jpg 300w, ../../wp-content/uploads/2024/08/Charles-Viertel-and-Lawrie-Hirst-1024x682.jpg 1024w, ../../wp-content/uploads/2024/08/Charles-Viertel-and-Lawrie-Hirst-768x512.jpg 768w, ../../wp-content/uploads/2024/08/Charles-Viertel-and-Lawrie-Hirst-1536x1023.jpg 1536w, ../../wp-content/uploads/2024/08/Charles-Viertel-and-Lawrie-Hirst-1430x953.jpg 1430w, ../../wp-content/uploads/2024/08/Charles-Viertel-and-Lawrie-Hirst-800x533.jpg 800w, ../../wp-content/uploads/2024/08/Charles-Viertel-and-Lawrie-Hirst-400x267.jpg 400w\" tabindex=\"-1\" width=\"1804\"/><figcaption class=\"wp-caption-text\" id=\"caption-attachment-5332\">Charles Viertel (L) with Professor Lawrence Hirst in 1986</figcaption></figure>\n<p>The new Chair, situated at the Princess Alexandra Hospital and filled by Professor Lawrence (Lawrie) Hirst, allowed for concentrated research efforts. Importantly, it also provided opportunities to influence curriculum and mentor students to become the next generation of vision scientists.</p>\n<h2>Queensland Eye Institute is born</h2>\n<p>Nearly 20 years later, in 2005, the Foundation took a different approach, opening its own research and education facility in a corner of the Mater Hospital. The new Queensland Eye Institute, with Professor Lawrie Hirst at the helm, included a small private clinic, offering a unique opportunity for ophthalmologists to pursue research interests while delivering cutting-edge clinical care to Queenslanders.</p>\n<figure aria-describedby=\"caption-attachment-5314\" class=\"wp-caption aligncenter\" id=\"attachment_5314\" style=\"width: 1379px\"><img alt='Large yellow brick building five storeys tall with a sign at front door reading \"Queensland Eye Institute\"' class=\"size-full wp-image-5314\" decoding=\"async\" height=\"903\" id=\"longdesc-return-5314\" loading=\"lazy\" longdesc=\"https://qei.org.au?longdesc=5314&amp;referrer=5312\" sizes=\"auto, (max-width: 1379px) 100vw, 1379px\" src=\"../../wp-content/uploads/2024/08/Mater-Hospital-first-QEI-premises.jpg\" srcset=\"../../wp-content/uploads/2024/08/Mater-Hospital-first-QEI-premises.jpg 1379w, ../../wp-content/uploads/2024/08/Mater-Hospital-first-QEI-premises-300x196.jpg 300w, ../../wp-content/uploads/2024/08/Mater-Hospital-first-QEI-premises-1024x671.jpg 1024w, ../../wp-content/uploads/2024/08/Mater-Hospital-first-QEI-premises-768x503.jpg 768w, ../../wp-content/uploads/2024/08/Mater-Hospital-first-QEI-premises-800x524.jpg 800w, ../../wp-content/uploads/2024/08/Mater-Hospital-first-QEI-premises-400x262.jpg 400w\" tabindex=\"-1\" width=\"1379\"/><figcaption class=\"wp-caption-text\" id=\"caption-attachment-5314\">The Queensland Eye Institute opened at the Mater Hospital on Annerley Road in 2005</figcaption></figure>\n<p>Some of the founding research ophthalmologists continue to anchor the QEI clinic. Dr Anthony Pane was the first to join QEI, and quickly recruited Dr Anthony Kwan, now an associate professor, from the United Kingdom. Over the years they’ve worked with an equally dedicated corporate team, which has provided best-practice governance and administration.</p>\n<figure aria-describedby=\"caption-attachment-5338\" class=\"wp-caption aligncenter\" id=\"attachment_5338\" style=\"width: 2000px\"><img alt=\"A group of men and women in suits with name tags inside a crowded room smile at the camera\" class=\"size-full wp-image-5338\" decoding=\"async\" height=\"1258\" id=\"longdesc-return-5338\" loading=\"lazy\" longdesc=\"https://qei.org.au?longdesc=5338&amp;referrer=5312\" sizes=\"auto, (max-width: 2000px) 100vw, 2000px\" src=\"../../wp-content/uploads/2024/08/Official-opening-QEI_Kelly-Langdon.jpg\" srcset=\"../../wp-content/uploads/2024/08/Official-opening-QEI_Kelly-Langdon.jpg 2000w, ../../wp-content/uploads/2024/08/Official-opening-QEI_Kelly-Langdon-300x189.jpg 300w, ../../wp-content/uploads/2024/08/Official-opening-QEI_Kelly-Langdon-1024x644.jpg 1024w, ../../wp-content/uploads/2024/08/Official-opening-QEI_Kelly-Langdon-768x483.jpg 768w, ../../wp-content/uploads/2024/08/Official-opening-QEI_Kelly-Langdon-1536x966.jpg 1536w, ../../wp-content/uploads/2024/08/Official-opening-QEI_Kelly-Langdon-1430x899.jpg 1430w, ../../wp-content/uploads/2024/08/Official-opening-QEI_Kelly-Langdon-800x503.jpg 800w, ../../wp-content/uploads/2024/08/Official-opening-QEI_Kelly-Langdon-400x252.jpg 400w\" tabindex=\"-1\" width=\"2000\"/><figcaption class=\"wp-caption-text\" id=\"caption-attachment-5338\">2005 QEI’s Chief Operation Officer Kelly Langdon (left front) with Professor Lawrie Hirst (right front) at the opening of the Queensland Eye Institute</figcaption></figure>\n<figure aria-describedby=\"caption-attachment-5333\" class=\"wp-caption aligncenter\" id=\"attachment_5333\" style=\"width: 2000px\"><img alt=\"A man holding a piece of paper stands talking to a group of people sitting in chairs in a room with a notice board on the wall\" class=\"size-full wp-image-5333\" decoding=\"async\" height=\"1333\" id=\"longdesc-return-5333\" loading=\"lazy\" longdesc=\"https://qei.org.au?longdesc=5333&amp;referrer=5312\" sizes=\"auto, (max-width: 2000px) 100vw, 2000px\" src=\"../../wp-content/uploads/2024/08/Dr-Anthony-Pane-2006.jpg\" srcset=\"../../wp-content/uploads/2024/08/Dr-Anthony-Pane-2006.jpg 2000w, ../../wp-content/uploads/2024/08/Dr-Anthony-Pane-2006-300x200.jpg 300w, ../../wp-content/uploads/2024/08/Dr-Anthony-Pane-2006-1024x682.jpg 1024w, ../../wp-content/uploads/2024/08/Dr-Anthony-Pane-2006-768x512.jpg 768w, ../../wp-content/uploads/2024/08/Dr-Anthony-Pane-2006-1536x1024.jpg 1536w, ../../wp-content/uploads/2024/08/Dr-Anthony-Pane-2006-1430x953.jpg 1430w, ../../wp-content/uploads/2024/08/Dr-Anthony-Pane-2006-800x533.jpg 800w, ../../wp-content/uploads/2024/08/Dr-Anthony-Pane-2006-400x267.jpg 400w\" tabindex=\"-1\" width=\"2000\"/><figcaption class=\"wp-caption-text\" id=\"caption-attachment-5333\">2006 QEI’s Dr Anthony Pane teaching students at QEI’s first home at the Mater Hospital</figcaption></figure>\n<figure aria-describedby=\"caption-attachment-5331\" class=\"wp-caption aligncenter\" id=\"attachment_5331\" style=\"width: 1314px\"><img alt=\"A man holding a folder looks at the screen of an eye examination screen while a woman looks into the eye piece on the machine\" class=\"wp-image-5331 size-full\" decoding=\"async\" height=\"876\" id=\"longdesc-return-5331\" loading=\"lazy\" longdesc=\"https://qei.org.au?longdesc=5331&amp;referrer=5312\" sizes=\"auto, (max-width: 1314px) 100vw, 1314px\" src=\"../../wp-content/uploads/2024/08/Anthony-Kwan-2008.jpg\" srcset=\"../../wp-content/uploads/2024/08/Anthony-Kwan-2008.jpg 1314w, ../../wp-content/uploads/2024/08/Anthony-Kwan-2008-300x200.jpg 300w, ../../wp-content/uploads/2024/08/Anthony-Kwan-2008-1024x683.jpg 1024w, ../../wp-content/uploads/2024/08/Anthony-Kwan-2008-768x512.jpg 768w, ../../wp-content/uploads/2024/08/Anthony-Kwan-2008-800x533.jpg 800w, ../../wp-content/uploads/2024/08/Anthony-Kwan-2008-400x267.jpg 400w\" tabindex=\"-1\" width=\"1314\"/><figcaption class=\"wp-caption-text\" id=\"caption-attachment-5331\">2008 Associate Professor Anthony Kwan at QEI’s clinic at the Mater Hospital</figcaption></figure>\n<figure aria-describedby=\"caption-attachment-5336\" class=\"wp-caption aligncenter\" id=\"attachment_5336\" style=\"width: 2000px\"><img alt=\"Two men smile at the camera while leaning over a box with a clear surface, lit from within, to look at slides\" class=\"wp-image-5336 size-full\" decoding=\"async\" height=\"1333\" id=\"longdesc-return-5336\" loading=\"lazy\" longdesc=\"https://qei.org.au?longdesc=5336&amp;referrer=5312\" sizes=\"auto, (max-width: 2000px) 100vw, 2000px\" src=\"../../wp-content/uploads/2024/08/Medical-students.jpg\" srcset=\"../../wp-content/uploads/2024/08/Medical-students.jpg 2000w, ../../wp-content/uploads/2024/08/Medical-students-300x200.jpg 300w, ../../wp-content/uploads/2024/08/Medical-students-1024x682.jpg 1024w, ../../wp-content/uploads/2024/08/Medical-students-768x512.jpg 768w, ../../wp-content/uploads/2024/08/Medical-students-1536x1024.jpg 1536w, ../../wp-content/uploads/2024/08/Medical-students-1430x953.jpg 1430w, ../../wp-content/uploads/2024/08/Medical-students-800x533.jpg 800w, ../../wp-content/uploads/2024/08/Medical-students-400x267.jpg 400w\" tabindex=\"-1\" width=\"2000\"/><figcaption class=\"wp-caption-text\" id=\"caption-attachment-5336\">2008 Dr David Gunn (L) as a fourth-year medical student at QEI at the Mater Hospital. His fellow student, Pat Glover, is now an anaesthetist at Gold Coast Hospital</figcaption></figure>\n<h2>The South Brisbane years</h2>\n<p>When Professor Mark Radford was appointed as Chief Executive Officer and Executive Director in 2010, he recognised the importance of sustainable funding for QEI’s research and education programs. Professor Radford oversaw QEI’s expansion into new premises at South Brisbane, which included two laboratories and an auditorium.</p>\n<figure aria-describedby=\"caption-attachment-5341\" class=\"wp-caption aligncenter\" id=\"attachment_5341\" style=\"width: 2000px\"><img alt=\"A woman in a red jacket stands in the centre with four men in dark suits beside her. They stand in front of brass plaque, mounted on a display board.\" class=\"wp-image-5341 size-full\" decoding=\"async\" height=\"1332\" id=\"longdesc-return-5341\" loading=\"lazy\" longdesc=\"https://qei.org.au?longdesc=5341&amp;referrer=5312\" sizes=\"auto, (max-width: 2000px) 100vw, 2000px\" src=\"../../wp-content/uploads/2024/08/QEI_opening_140MelbourneStSouthBrisbane.jpg\" srcset=\"../../wp-content/uploads/2024/08/QEI_opening_140MelbourneStSouthBrisbane.jpg 2000w, ../../wp-content/uploads/2024/08/QEI_opening_140MelbourneStSouthBrisbane-300x200.jpg 300w, ../../wp-content/uploads/2024/08/QEI_opening_140MelbourneStSouthBrisbane-1024x682.jpg 1024w, ../../wp-content/uploads/2024/08/QEI_opening_140MelbourneStSouthBrisbane-768x511.jpg 768w, ../../wp-content/uploads/2024/08/QEI_opening_140MelbourneStSouthBrisbane-1536x1023.jpg 1536w, ../../wp-content/uploads/2024/08/QEI_opening_140MelbourneStSouthBrisbane-1430x952.jpg 1430w, ../../wp-content/uploads/2024/08/QEI_opening_140MelbourneStSouthBrisbane-800x533.jpg 800w, ../../wp-content/uploads/2024/08/QEI_opening_140MelbourneStSouthBrisbane-400x266.jpg 400w\" tabindex=\"-1\" width=\"2000\"/><figcaption class=\"wp-caption-text\" id=\"caption-attachment-5341\">2014 Official opening of QEI’s new premises in Melbourne Street, South Brisbane (L-R) Professor Mark Radford, Rex Freudenberg, then Governor of Queensland, The Honourable Penelope Wensley AC, George Curphy and Chair of Queensland Eye Institute Foundation Mark Sheridan</figcaption></figure>\n<figure aria-describedby=\"caption-attachment-5342\" class=\"wp-caption aligncenter\" id=\"attachment_5342\" style=\"width: 2000px\"><img alt=\"A woman in a red jacket talks to two men, standing in front of a banner advertising the Queensland Eye Institute\" class=\"wp-image-5342 size-full\" decoding=\"async\" height=\"1333\" id=\"longdesc-return-5342\" loading=\"lazy\" longdesc=\"https://qei.org.au?longdesc=5342&amp;referrer=5312\" sizes=\"auto, (max-width: 2000px) 100vw, 2000px\" src=\"../../wp-content/uploads/2024/08/QEI_opening_140MelbourneStSouthBrisbane-1.jpg\" srcset=\"../../wp-content/uploads/2024/08/QEI_opening_140MelbourneStSouthBrisbane-1.jpg 2000w, ../../wp-content/uploads/2024/08/QEI_opening_140MelbourneStSouthBrisbane-1-300x200.jpg 300w, ../../wp-content/uploads/2024/08/QEI_opening_140MelbourneStSouthBrisbane-1-1024x682.jpg 1024w, ../../wp-content/uploads/2024/08/QEI_opening_140MelbourneStSouthBrisbane-1-768x512.jpg 768w, ../../wp-content/uploads/2024/08/QEI_opening_140MelbourneStSouthBrisbane-1-1536x1024.jpg 1536w, ../../wp-content/uploads/2024/08/QEI_opening_140MelbourneStSouthBrisbane-1-1430x953.jpg 1430w, ../../wp-content/uploads/2024/08/QEI_opening_140MelbourneStSouthBrisbane-1-800x533.jpg 800w, ../../wp-content/uploads/2024/08/QEI_opening_140MelbourneStSouthBrisbane-1-400x267.jpg 400w\" tabindex=\"-1\" width=\"2000\"/><figcaption class=\"wp-caption-text\" id=\"caption-attachment-5342\">2014 Then Governor of Queensland, The Honourable Penelope Wensley AC, Professor Mark Radford and Dr Brendan Cronin tour the Queensland Eye Institute’s microsurgical workshop at South Brisbane</figcaption></figure>\n<div class=\"wp-block-image\"></div>\n<div></div>\n<figure aria-describedby=\"caption-attachment-5340\" class=\"wp-caption aligncenter\" id=\"attachment_5340\" style=\"width: 2000px\"><img alt=\"Group of 10 men and women in short white coats, standing with their hands folded in front of them in a laboratory.\" class=\"wpa-warning wpa-image-missing-alt wp-image-5340 size-full\" data-warning=\"Missing alt text\" decoding=\"async\" height=\"1333\" loading=\"lazy\" sizes=\"auto, (max-width: 2000px) 100vw, 2000px\" src=\"../../wp-content/uploads/2024/08/QEI-scientists.jpg\" srcset=\"../../wp-content/uploads/2024/08/QEI-scientists.jpg 2000w, ../../wp-content/uploads/2024/08/QEI-scientists-300x200.jpg 300w, ../../wp-content/uploads/2024/08/QEI-scientists-1024x682.jpg 1024w, ../../wp-content/uploads/2024/08/QEI-scientists-768x512.jpg 768w, ../../wp-content/uploads/2024/08/QEI-scientists-1536x1024.jpg 1536w, ../../wp-content/uploads/2024/08/QEI-scientists-1430x953.jpg 1430w, ../../wp-content/uploads/2024/08/QEI-scientists-800x533.jpg 800w, ../../wp-content/uploads/2024/08/QEI-scientists-400x267.jpg 400w\" width=\"2000\"/><figcaption class=\"wp-caption-text\" id=\"caption-attachment-5340\">QEI’s Chief Scientist Professor Traian Chirila (centre) with QEI scientists at the South Brisbane laboratory</figcaption></figure>\n<p>At the same time, with the support of the Sylvia and Charles Viertel Foundation, the renamed Queensland Eye Institute Foundation built a dedicated day hospital. The profit-for-purpose facility, co-located with QEI, serviced its growing clinical faculty and raised funds to support QEI’s research program.</p>\n<figure aria-describedby=\"caption-attachment-5343\" class=\"wp-caption aligncenter\" id=\"attachment_5343\" style=\"width: 1791px\"><img alt='A tall building with large glass doors and concrete pillars with a sign at the front reading \"Queensland Eye Institute and South Bank Day Hospital\"' class=\"size-full wp-image-5343\" decoding=\"async\" height=\"1194\" id=\"longdesc-return-5343\" loading=\"lazy\" longdesc=\"https://qei.org.au?longdesc=5343&amp;referrer=5312\" sizes=\"auto, (max-width: 1791px) 100vw, 1791px\" src=\"../../wp-content/uploads/2024/08/South-Brisbane-Day-Hospital.jpg\" srcset=\"../../wp-content/uploads/2024/08/South-Brisbane-Day-Hospital.jpg 1791w, ../../wp-content/uploads/2024/08/South-Brisbane-Day-Hospital-300x200.jpg 300w, ../../wp-content/uploads/2024/08/South-Brisbane-Day-Hospital-1024x683.jpg 1024w, ../../wp-content/uploads/2024/08/South-Brisbane-Day-Hospital-768x512.jpg 768w, ../../wp-content/uploads/2024/08/South-Brisbane-Day-Hospital-1536x1024.jpg 1536w, ../../wp-content/uploads/2024/08/South-Brisbane-Day-Hospital-1430x953.jpg 1430w, ../../wp-content/uploads/2024/08/South-Brisbane-Day-Hospital-800x533.jpg 800w, ../../wp-content/uploads/2024/08/South-Brisbane-Day-Hospital-400x267.jpg 400w\" tabindex=\"-1\" width=\"1791\"/><figcaption class=\"wp-caption-text\" id=\"caption-attachment-5343\">Queensland Eye Institute and South Bank Day Hospital at 140 Melbourne Street, South Brisbane</figcaption></figure>\n<h2>A bright future at Woolloongabba</h2>\n<p>The next chapter in QEI’s evolution has commenced with this latest move to 87 Ipswich Road Woolloongabba. The new building provides more space for its expanding clinical services and more efficient facilities for research and administration teams. The new building also supports QEI’s contemporary education program, research projects and collaborations with fewer overheads and less waste.</p>\n<figure aria-describedby=\"caption-attachment-5335\" class=\"wp-caption aligncenter\" id=\"attachment_5335\" style=\"width: 2000px\"><img alt=\"A man stands over a group of people looking through microscopes, pointing to the equipment and talking\" class=\"size-full wp-image-5335\" decoding=\"async\" height=\"1333\" id=\"longdesc-return-5335\" loading=\"lazy\" longdesc=\"https://qei.org.au?longdesc=5335&amp;referrer=5312\" sizes=\"auto, (max-width: 2000px) 100vw, 2000px\" src=\"../../wp-content/uploads/2024/08/Lee-Holland-teaching.jpg\" srcset=\"../../wp-content/uploads/2024/08/Lee-Holland-teaching.jpg 2000w, ../../wp-content/uploads/2024/08/Lee-Holland-teaching-300x200.jpg 300w, ../../wp-content/uploads/2024/08/Lee-Holland-teaching-1024x682.jpg 1024w, ../../wp-content/uploads/2024/08/Lee-Holland-teaching-768x512.jpg 768w, ../../wp-content/uploads/2024/08/Lee-Holland-teaching-1536x1024.jpg 1536w, ../../wp-content/uploads/2024/08/Lee-Holland-teaching-1430x953.jpg 1430w, ../../wp-content/uploads/2024/08/Lee-Holland-teaching-800x533.jpg 800w, ../../wp-content/uploads/2024/08/Lee-Holland-teaching-400x267.jpg 400w\" tabindex=\"-1\" width=\"2000\"/><figcaption class=\"wp-caption-text\" id=\"caption-attachment-5335\">2024 QEI’s Dr Lee Holland instructing optometrists at an education event held at the new Woolloongabba premises</figcaption></figure>\n<p>There are now 10 sub-specialist ophthalmologists practising at QEI, covering everything from eyelid, cornea, retina, glaucoma and neuro ophthalmology. The depth and breadth of clinical practice ensure patient needs can be met by more than one clinician and give the doctors more opportunity to pursue research.</p>\n<p>The building’s intelligent and energy-efficient design will save on running costs, ensuring more of the Foundation’s resources can be channelled into its research, just as its founders had envisaged.</p>\n<figure aria-describedby=\"caption-attachment-5330\" class=\"wp-caption aligncenter\" id=\"attachment_5330\" style=\"width: 2000px\"><img alt=\"A four storey building with red brick on the ground floor and white cladding above with dark tinted windows. There are sings on the building reading &quot;87 Ipswich Rd, Queensland Eye Institute, Viertel's Vision, South Brisbane Day Hospital&quot;\" class=\"size-full wp-image-5330\" decoding=\"async\" height=\"1334\" id=\"longdesc-return-5330\" loading=\"lazy\" longdesc=\"https://qei.org.au?longdesc=5330&amp;referrer=5312\" sizes=\"auto, (max-width: 2000px) 100vw, 2000px\" src=\"../../wp-content/uploads/2024/08/2024020187IpswichRd4.jpg\" srcset=\"../../wp-content/uploads/2024/08/2024020187IpswichRd4.jpg 2000w, ../../wp-content/uploads/2024/08/2024020187IpswichRd4-300x200.jpg 300w, ../../wp-content/uploads/2024/08/2024020187IpswichRd4-1024x683.jpg 1024w, ../../wp-content/uploads/2024/08/2024020187IpswichRd4-768x512.jpg 768w, ../../wp-content/uploads/2024/08/2024020187IpswichRd4-1536x1025.jpg 1536w, ../../wp-content/uploads/2024/08/2024020187IpswichRd4-1430x954.jpg 1430w, ../../wp-content/uploads/2024/08/2024020187IpswichRd4-800x534.jpg 800w, ../../wp-content/uploads/2024/08/2024020187IpswichRd4-400x267.jpg 400w\" tabindex=\"-1\" width=\"2000\"/><figcaption class=\"wp-caption-text\" id=\"caption-attachment-5330\">2024 Queensland Eye Institute and South Brisbane Day Hospital at 87 Ipswich Rd, Woolloongabba</figcaption></figure>",
